[
    {
        "content": "<p>We want to implement <code>AffineSubspace.rank</code>. We cannot define it as <code>Module.rank k s.direction</code> because this assigns rank 0 to both the empty subspace and singleton subspaces. This is fine for singletons, but is not what we usually expect for the empty subspace:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> What should be the dimension of the empty subspace?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Any opinions for what is the best choice mathematically and implementation-wise are welcome.</p>",
        "id": 619115381,
        "sender_full_name": "Martin Winter",
        "timestamp": 1787687507
    },
    {
        "content": "<h6>Option 1: <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>dim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∅</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dim(\\varnothing )= -1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\">dim</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">∅</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">−</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span></h6>\n<p>This is the dimension we classically assign to e.g. the empty polytope. There are also cases where this makes sense arithmetically. For example, <a href=\"#narrow/channel/116395-maths/topic/Homogenization.20of.20.60AffineSpace.60/with/604261703\">affine homogenization</a> of the empty set yields the trivial subspace <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{0\\}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">{</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mclose\">}</span></span></span></span> whose dimension is zero and should be exactly one higher than before homogenization. Also, the join of two spaces of dimension <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8444em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8444em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> has dimension <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_1+d_2+1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8444em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8444em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span>, which works correctly with this convention.</p>\n<p>I do not see how to implement this for <code>rank</code> which is <code>Cardinal</code>-valued. <code>finrank</code> could be made <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb Z</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Z</span></span></span></span>-valued. In the latter case, should the junk value assigned to infinite subspaces still be zero?</p>",
        "id": 619115477,
        "sender_full_name": "Martin Winter",
        "timestamp": 1787687523
    },
    {
        "content": "<h6>Option 2: <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>dim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∅</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dim(\\varnothing )= -\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\">dim</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">∅</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">−</span><span class=\"mord\">∞</span></span></span></span></h6>\n<p>This works particularly well with products: the product of spaces of dimensions <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8444em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8444em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> has dimension <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_1+d_2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8444em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8444em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>; if one of the spaces is empty, the product is empty, which works because <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">−</span><span class=\"mord\">∞</span></span></span></span> is absorbing.</p>\n<p>This could be implemented using <code>WithBot Cardinal</code> resp. <code>WithBot Nat</code>.</p>",
        "id": 619115503,
        "sender_full_name": "Martin Winter",
        "timestamp": 1787687529
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Maybe a mixed approach could work, where we use <code>WithBot Cardinal</code> for <code>rank</code> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Bbb Z</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Z</span></span></span></span> for <code>finrank</code>. Not sure whether this will make problems later on.</p>",
        "id": 619115510,
        "sender_full_name": "Martin Winter",
        "timestamp": 1787687532
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I would suggest using a <code>WithBot</code>, this is what we do for e.g. <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Polynomial.degree#doc\">docs#Polynomial.degree</a></p>",
        "id": 619118550,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1787688383
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You can use <code>.succ</code> to get the additive behavior in option 1 and <code>+ 1</code> to get the absorbing behavior in option 2 using <code>WithBot</code></p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">⊥</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WithBot</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">succ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">⊥</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WithBot</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">⊥</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WithBot</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 619375019,
        "sender_full_name": "Vlad Tsyrklevich",
        "timestamp": 1787775094
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Hm. I am still skeptical that we get the nice arithmetic (beyond just succesors) that is needed for expressing certain theorems.</p>",
        "id": 619996211,
        "sender_full_name": "Martin Winter",
        "timestamp": 1788017216
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Also, <code>Order.succ</code> does not fix infinite cardinals and therefore does not reflect the behavior of dimension under homogenization. But this raises a different questions:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Does anyone care about infinite dimensional affine spaces?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Otherwise we could just work with <code>finrank</code> and make it <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Bbb Z</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Z</span></span></span></span>-valued.</p>",
        "id": 619996332,
        "sender_full_name": "Martin Winter",
        "timestamp": 1788017303
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>why do we have both <code>WithBot.succ</code> and an <code>OrderSucc</code> instance which do the same thing</p>",
        "id": 619998379,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1788018751
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>They don't have the same type</p>",
        "id": 619998405,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1788018777
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>oh <code>WithBot.succ</code> returns an element of the base type</p>",
        "id": 619998414,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1788018783
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"816344\">Aaron Liu</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Dimensions.20of.20affine.20subspaces/near/619118550\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Polynomial.degree#doc\">docs#Polynomial.degree</a></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If you have worked with it, can you answer this: does this lead to a lot of case distinctions in applications/proofs? This is what I hope can be avoided by a good definition.</p>",
        "id": 620006302,
        "sender_full_name": "Martin Winter",
        "timestamp": 1788024544
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>well we also have <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Polynomial.natDegree#doc\">docs#Polynomial.natDegree</a> :)</p>",
        "id": 620006969,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1788024870
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I haven't every had to make an argument by cases but I have had to show <code>Polynomial.natDegree p ≠ 0</code> very much</p>",
        "id": 620007095,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1788024928
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"816344\">Aaron Liu</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Dimensions.20of.20affine.20subspaces/near/620006969\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>well we also have <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Polynomial.natDegree#doc\">docs#Polynomial.natDegree</a> :)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This seems to be the analogue of <code>Module.rank k s.direction</code>, which we want to avoid <span aria-label=\"grimacing\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f62c\" role=\"img\" title=\"grimacing\">:grimacing:</span></p>",
        "id": 620007322,
        "sender_full_name": "Martin Winter",
        "timestamp": 1788025052
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Sometimes you might want to provide both versions even if it leads to API duplication</p>",
        "id": 620012226,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1788027632
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>though I guess in this case having a inlined <code>Module.rank k s.direction</code> is good enough if we need the Nat version</p>",
        "id": 620012384,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1788027709
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"873350\">Weiyi Wang</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Dimensions.20of.20affine.20subspaces/near/620012384\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>though I guess in this case having a inlined <code>Module.rank k s.direction</code> is good enough if we need the Nat version</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I don't understand. <code>Module.rank k s.direction</code> is definitely not good enough because it gives the wrong rank for the empty subspace.</p>",
        "id": 620023303,
        "sender_full_name": "Martin Winter",
        "timestamp": 1788034823
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't mean using <code>Module.rank k s.direction</code> as the primary definition, and I still support <code>WithBot</code> (or <code>Int</code>. I like it less but I am still ok with it). The point I was trying to make is that Mathlib also has a lot of secondary definition (<code>Module.finrank</code>, <code>Polynomial.natDegree</code>...) which are not fully general but is easy to use when one just wants a <code>Nat</code></p>",
        "id": 620029749,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1788040244
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Sometimes these secondary definitions also have some good properties due to happy accidents on the junk values</p>",
        "id": 620029865,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1788040339
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Now that a dimension for affine spaces was merged (<a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43270\">#43270</a>) I have some comments:</p>\n<h3>Dimension arithmetic</h3>\n<p>I wonder in how far we can do dimension arithmetics. Each interval [a,b] in the lattice L of affine subspaces is also a lattice of affine subspaces to a suitable affine space. So if we  have an affine subspace S in [a,b], then dim_L(S) = dim_L(a) + dim_[a,b] (S). This does not work with the arithmetic we currently have on <code>WithBot Nat</code>, because <code>bot</code> is absorbing when using +.</p>",
        "id": 621638280,
        "sender_full_name": "Martin Winter",
        "timestamp": 1788510698
    },
    {
        "content": "<h3><code>FiniteDimensional</code> and other implementation details</h3>\n<p>The fact that affine dimension is implemented using the rank of its module of directions is an implementation detail. But it is still exposed in lemmas such as <code>dim_lt_aleph0</code> because it assumes <code>[Module.Finite R s.direction]</code>. Rather we should have an affine analogue of <code>FiniteDimensional</code>. Likely for other notions as well.</p>",
        "id": 621638298,
        "sender_full_name": "Martin Winter",
        "timestamp": 1788510703
    },
    {
        "content": "<h3>Dimension of <code>AffineSpace</code></h3>\n<p>We need parallel API for expressing the dimension of the <code>AffineSpace</code> itself (going via the top subspace is annoying). Initially I thought that <code>dim</code> should have been defined on <code>AffineSpace</code> rather than <code>AffineSubspace</code>, like it is the case for modules. First, this would not work as nicely, because general affine subspaces are not affine spaces because the latter cannot be empty (the reason for this is probably documented somewhere). But <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"387244\">@Yaël Dillies</span> told me that we actually don't want affine subspaces to use the affine space API anyway, and that the current implementation for modules is wrong and needs to change. So the consequence is we need API duplication.</p>",
        "id": 621638302,
        "sender_full_name": "Martin Winter",
        "timestamp": 1788510705
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>There's been some previous discussion of changing <code>AddTorsor</code> (and, now, <code>Torsor</code>) to \"pre-torsors\" (removing the <code>Nonempty</code> requirement and instead adding it to those lemmas / definitions that need it).</p>",
